::PC_U04_L03_GP01::
Question ID: PC_U04_L03_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.3
Question:
Which identity is a fundamental Pythagorean identity?
{
~A. sin(x)+cos(x)=1#Sine and cosine do not add to 1 for all values.
=B. sin^2(x)+cos^2(x)=1#Correct. The unit circle gives sin^2(x)+cos^2(x)=1.
~C. tan(x)+1=sec(x)#This confuses the squared tangent identity with a linear expression.
~D. sin^2(x)-cos^2(x)=1#The subtraction form is not the fundamental Pythagorean identity.
}
::PC_U04_L03_GP02::
Question ID: PC_U04_L03_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.3
Question:
If sin(x)=3/5 and x is in Quadrant I, what is cos(x)?
{
~A. 3/5#This repeats the sine value instead of finding cosine.
~B. 9/25#This is sin^2(x), not cosine.
~C. 16/25#This is cos^2(x), not cos(x).
=D. 4/5#Correct. cos^2(x)=1-9/25=16/25, so cos(x)=4/5 in Quadrant I.
}
::PC_U04_L03_GP03::
Question ID: PC_U04_L03_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.3
Question:
Which identity connects tangent and secant?
{
=A. 1+tan^2(x)=sec^2(x)#Correct. Dividing sin^2(x)+cos^2(x)=1 by cos^2(x) gives 1+tan^2(x)=sec^2(x).
~B. 1+sec^2(x)=tan^2(x)#The terms are reversed incorrectly.
~C. sin^2(x)+tan^2(x)=1#This mixes sine and tangent in a false identity.
~D. cos(x)+sec(x)=1#Cosine and secant are reciprocals, not values that add to 1.
}
::PC_U04_L03_GP04::
Question ID: PC_U04_L03_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.3
Question:
What does it mean to prove a trigonometric identity?
{
~A. Check one number only#One example does not prove an identity for all allowed values.
~B. Use a calculator for both sides#A calculator check is not a proof.
=C. Show equivalent steps that make one side match the other#Correct. A proof uses justified equivalent transformations.
~D. Change the equation until it looks easier#Steps must preserve equivalence and be justified.
}
::PC_U04_L03_GP05::
Question ID: PC_U04_L03_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.3
Question:
If tan(x)=4/3 in Quadrant I, what is sec(x)?
{
~A. 4/3#This repeats tangent instead of finding secant.
=B. 5/3#Correct. 1+tan^2(x)=1+16/9=25/9, so sec(x)=5/3 in Quadrant I.
~C. 3/5#This is cosine, the reciprocal of secant.
~D. 25/9#This is sec^2(x), not sec(x).
}